What is a Program?
	A program is basically:
		Data + Operations on that data



struct Student {
    string name;
    int age;
    float marks;
};


void add_student(Student&);
void update_marks(Student&);
void calculate_grade(Student&);
void display_student(const Student&);
void save_student(const Student&);




                 PROGRAM
                    |
          +---------+---------+
          |                   |
        DATA              OPERATIONS
          |                   |
       Student          add_student()
                        update_marks()
                        calculate_grade()
                        display_student()
                        save_student()





What is Procedural Programming?
	In procedural programming, we primarily organize the program around functions/procedures.


The basic thinking is:
	What operations do I need to perform?
             ↓
	Create functions
             ↓
	Pass data to those functions


Example:
	Student s;

	update_marks(s);
	calculate_grade(s);
	display_student(s);



These functions operate on the data.
	This approach is perfectly valid.

For example, C is procedural and is heavily used in:
	Embedded systems
	Device drivers
	Operating systems
	Firmware
	Networking
	Real-time systems



What Problems Appear as the Program Grows?
	1. Data and Functions Are Separated
		The data is in one place.
		The operations are somewhere else.


		As the code base increaes, Which functions belong to this data? Which functions are allowed to modify it?
		Which function is responsible for maintaining its validity?



	2. Data Representation Changes
		struct Student {
    			string name;
    			int age;
    			vector<float> subject_marks;
		};


		Now functions that depended on the old representation may need modification:
		
		Internal data representation changes
                ↓
		Functions depending on that representation
                ↓
		Multiple functions may need modification	
		

	3. Data Is Exposed
		struct BankAccount {
    			double balance;
		};

		BankAccount account;
			account.balance = -50000;
		The internal state is directly exposed to the outside world.

	4. Uncontrolled Modification of Shared Data

	    Shared Data
                 |
      +----------+----------+
      |          |          |
   Function A Function B Function C
      |          |          |
      +----------+----------+
                 |
           modifies data



Any of these functions might change the same data.
	As the system grows, debugging becomes difficult because a change made in one place can affect behavior somewhere else.

This creates:

	Hidden dependencies
	Side effects
	Difficult debugging
	Difficult testing


	5. Tight Coupling
		Suppose many functions know exactly how Student is internally represented.
		
	s.name
	s.age
	s.marks

	Those functions are now coupled to the implementation of Student.

What if we could keep the data and the operations that belong to that data together?




Object-Oriented Programming:
	1. What Is a Class?
		A class allows us to define a new type containing:
			->Data members
			->Member functions


class Student {
    string name;
    int age;
    float marks;

    void update_marks(float new_marks) {
        marks = new_marks;
    }

    void display() {
        cout << name << " "
             << age << " "
             << marks << endl;
    }
};

                 Student
                    |
        +-----------+-----------+
        |                       |
    DATA MEMBERS          MEMBER FUNCTIONS
        |                       |
      name                update_marks()
      age                 display()
      marks


A class combines related data and behavior into a single type.


Encapsulation: Encapsulating (bundling) related data and 
the functions that operate on that data into a single entity, typically a class.



	2. What Is an Object?
		The class is the type/definition.
		We create actual objects from that class:

		Student s1;
		Student s2;



Student  → class/type

s1       → object
s2       → object


Each object has its own state:

             Student class
                  |
          +-------+-------+
          |               |
         s1              s2
       Object          Object
          |               |
      name=Ravi       name=Priya
      age=21          age=22
      marks=85        marks=92

And each object can perform its behavior:
	s1.display();
	s2.display();

	s1.update_marks(90);

In the bove class:
	everything is private by default

Student
│
├── private
│   ├── name
│   ├── age
│   ├── marks
│   ├── update_marks()
│   └── display()


We cannot do:
	Student s;

	s.name = "Ravi";       // ERROR
	s.age = 20;            // ERROR
	s.marks = 90;          // ERROR
	s.update_marks(95);    // ERROR
	s.display();           // ERROR


We have hidden everything, but the class isn't useful to outside code yet.



Now expose selected functionality
-------------------------------------------
class Student {
private:
    string name;
    int age;
    float marks;

public:
    void update_marks(float new_marks) {
        marks = new_marks;
    }

    void display() {
        cout << name << " "
             << age << " "
             << marks << endl;
    }
};


                    Student
               +----------------+
               |                |
               |    PRIVATE     |
               |                |
               | name           |
               | age            |
               | marks          |
               |                |
               +-------+--------+
                       |
                 controlled access
                       |
               +-------+--------+
               |                |
               |    PUBLIC      |
               |                |
               | update_marks() |
               | display()      |
               |                |
               +----------------+



Outside code can now do:
	Student s;

	s.update_marks(90);  // OK
	s.display();         // OK


But 
	s.marks = 90;        // ERROR


C++ Access Specifiers:
-------------------------------

class Student {
private:
    string name;

protected:
    int age;

public:
    void display();
};




| Access specifier | Meaning                                                  |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| `private`        | Accessible only within the class (and permitted friends) |
| `protected`      | Accessible within the class and derived classes          |
| `public`         | Accessible from outside through an object                |



-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Constructors: 
	A constructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an object is created.
	Its main purpose is to initialize the object's data members.
	
	A constructor has the following characteristics:
		1. The constructor name must be exactly the same as the class name.

		Example: class MissionWallet
			 {
				public:
  		  			MissionWallet();   // Constructor
			 };

	       2. A constructor does not have a return type.
		  Not even void.
		Example: MissionWallet();       // Correct
			 void MissionWallet();  // Wrong		
	      
               3. A constructor is called automatically when an object is created.
		Example: MissionWallet wallet;


	       4. A constructor is normally declared in the class and defined outside the class.
		  when using separate .h and .cpp files.

		Example:     //.h file:
 
			class MissionWallet
			{
				public:
				    MissionWallet();
			};	
 		
	
			     //.CPP
			MissionWallet::MissionWallet()
			{
    				owner = "Unknown";
    				credits = 0.0;
			}

		
                5. A constructor can take parameters.
		Example: MissionWallet(std::string name, double initial_credits);

		MissionWallet::MissionWallet(std::string name, double initial_credits)
    			: owner(name), credits(initial_credits)  //: is called a member initializer list
		{

		} 

	        6. A constructor can be overloaded.

		7. A constructor cannot be called like a normal function to create an object. 
		   It is invoked as part of object construction.

		8. Constructors are not inherited in the traditional sense by derived classes, 
		   although modern C++ provides mechanisms such as using Base::Base to make base 
                   constructors available to derived-class construction.

Constructor
    |
    +-- Same name as class
    |
    +-- No return type
    |
    +-- Called automatically
    |
    +-- Called when object is created
    |
    +-- Initializes object
    |
    +-- Can have parameters
    |
    +-- Can be overloaded	



default constructor: Is a constructor that can be called without providing any arguments.

When a constructor is not created by user, default constructer is created by the compiler automatically. 

The best practice is to provide user define no args constructor.

Note: If a class contains a user-defined constructor, the compiler will not automatically generate a default (no-argument) 
constructor. If a default constructor is required, the user must define it explicitly or use = default.

If a constructor is created with arguments, we must always create an object with argument.

->We can have multiple constructures, but each constructor must have a unique signature.

============================================================================================================
Destructor: A destructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an 
object is destroyed, to perform cleanup of resources used by the object.

	Same name as class, preceded by a tilde (~).
 
	They are special mebmber methods.

	Invoked automatically when thr object is destroyed.

	No return type or no arguments required.


	Same name as class, preceded by a tilde (~).
 

	Destructor overloading is not allowed, we can only have one destructor.

	Example: ~MissionRobot();


Delegating Constructors:
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Multiple constructors can sometimes contain duplicate initialization code.
2. Duplicate code is undesirable because if the initialization logic changes, we may need to update it in multiple places.
3. C++ allows one constructor to call another constructor of the same class.
4. This is called a delegating constructor.
5. The constructor that delegates does not perform the initialization itself. Instead, it passes 
   the responsibility to another constructor.
6. Delegation is specified in the constructor's initialization list.
7. Delegating constructors help:

	Avoid code duplication
	Keep initialization logic in one place
	Reduce maintenance effort
	Reduce the possibility of inconsistent initialization
	Make overloaded constructors easier to manage








	
